WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:27.680
A hot Jupiter 1000 light years away has tilted out of Earth's view. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 254. Euclid's ancient quasars, a new reconstruction of the M87 ring, and moon dust bricks come next, then cosmic spotlight, a cosmic deep dive, and a weakened review. T-O-I-2535-B, watched by tests at the National Aeronautics and Space Administration, or NASA. According to Mashable,

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:33.760
across its star repeatedly for seven years. A hot Jupiter is a giant planet that orbits close

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.800
to its star, and a transit is that planet passing in front of the star as seen from Earth.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:45.120
Those transits came as much as six hours early or late, then turned shorter and dimmer and ceased

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:50.240
altogether earlier this year, as the path drifted from the star's center out toward the rim.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:57.000
An unseen companion at least as massive as Saturn itself on a tilted orbit is tugging the planet,

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.460
it, and the pair will keep pulling on one another.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:05.320
T-O-I-2535-B is expected to come back into view in 30 to 50 years.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:10.300
The planet's orbit is tilting so quickly that its transit disappeared while we were watching,

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.660
said Samuel Yee of UCLA.

00:01:13.858 --> 00:01:19.298
Euclid, the telescope of the European Space Agency, or ESA, according to Science Daily,

00:01:19.878 --> 00:01:24.958
identified 31 quasars from when the universe was only 670 million years old.

00:01:24.958 --> 00:01:29.658
A quasar is a brilliant core of a young galaxy powered by a black hole.

00:01:30.358 --> 00:01:34.158
Redshift is a measure of how much the universe has stretched an object's light.

00:01:34.638 --> 00:01:40.338
Two of the objects set new redshift records at 7.77 and 7.69.

00:01:41.058 --> 00:01:45.658
Both were as bright as a trillion suns and sit a little more than 13 billion light years

00:01:45.658 --> 00:01:46.218
from Earth.

00:01:46.818 --> 00:01:50.898
This finding more than doubles the number of quasars we know of that are so ancient,

00:01:50.898 --> 00:01:58.585
said Antonio Lamarca of ESA, closure invariants were applied, according to Fizz.org, to original

00:01:58.585 --> 00:02:02.045
event horizon telescope data of the M87 black hole.

00:02:02.665 --> 00:02:07.125
The method folds measurements from telescope triplets so atmospheric and instrumental

00:02:07.125 --> 00:02:08.225
errors drop out.

00:02:08.805 --> 00:02:15.025
A machine learning imaging approach called GenDIRCT, trained on ordinary photographs, then

00:02:15.025 --> 00:02:16.245
produced the reconstruction.

00:02:16.245 --> 00:02:24.965
The ring's diameter and orientation agree with the 2019 result for a black hole of 6.5 billion solar masses seen nearly face on.

00:02:25.585 --> 00:02:28.925
With and brightness of the ring differ a little from those earlier pictures.

00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:40.983
University of Arkansas researchers use laser powder bed fusion, according to Citech Daily, on Lunar Highlands Regolith Simulant LHS-1, a stand-in for Highland Moon Dust.

00:02:41.603 --> 00:02:45.643
The process heats chosen patches of powder until the grains fuse to each other.

00:02:45.643 --> 00:02:51.163
They printed blocks a few centimeters across, in cross and eight shapes, that lock together

00:02:51.163 --> 00:02:51.803
like Lego.

00:02:52.403 --> 00:02:56.003
The material is more full of pores than prints that use an added binder.

00:02:56.583 --> 00:03:01.123
Vacuum and microgravity tests still have to come before the bricks can carry structural

00:03:01.123 --> 00:03:01.563
loads.

00:03:02.555 --> 00:03:07.995
Beyond Pluto, a potentially enormous icy body has been detected, according to Science Daily,

00:03:08.275 --> 00:03:13.855
in a crowded region of the outer solar system, and designated 2026 ED 138.

00:03:13.855 --> 00:03:17.075
The object was hidden for years before its discovery.

00:03:17.755 --> 00:03:21.815
Confirmation would expand the solar system's official dwarf planet count to six.

00:03:22.435 --> 00:03:26.715
The fine also suggests more large worlds may remain undetected beyond Neptune.

00:03:27.846 --> 00:03:33.646
Long period radio transients have been traced, according to Fizz.org, since 2022 to pairs

00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:35.866
containing a white dwarf and a red dwarf.

00:03:36.346 --> 00:03:41.386
A white dwarf is a collapsed stellar remnant, and a red dwarf is a small, cool star.

00:03:41.386 --> 00:03:46.566
The systems produce bright radio flashes from a few minutes apart to a few hours apart.

00:03:47.086 --> 00:03:51.646
The white dwarfs magnetic field and interaction with the companion star are thought to drive

00:03:51.646 --> 00:03:52.226
the emission.

00:03:52.866 --> 00:03:57.026
More of these objects keep turning up as low frequency surveys get more sensitive.

00:03:58.148 --> 00:04:03.088
NASA's Voyager 1 is closing in on one light day from Earth as it travels through interstellar

00:04:03.088 --> 00:04:03.388
space.

00:04:04.048 --> 00:04:06.628
One light day is the distance light travels in a day.

00:04:07.268 --> 00:04:11.568
The spacecraft still sends back measurements of the heliopause and of the interstellar

00:04:11.568 --> 00:04:15.208
medium. No other craft built by humans has gone this far.

00:04:16.097 --> 00:04:21.437
Supernova SN Areas was split into multiple images by gravitational lensing from a massive

00:04:21.437 --> 00:04:27.837
galaxy cluster when the universe was 4.9 billion years old. A Supernova is a star exploding

00:04:27.837 --> 00:04:34.057
at the end of its life. The James Webb Space Telescope, or JWST, recorded those distorted

00:04:34.057 --> 00:04:39.937
views at high resolution. A paper posted to archive on October 23rd forecast the arrival

00:04:39.937 --> 00:04:43.037
of the remaining images, roughly 70 years apart.

00:04:43.677 --> 00:04:49.457
In brief, Jupiter and Mars, according to Space.com, appear just over one degree a part overnight

00:04:49.457 --> 00:04:53.937
and will make a close approach visible to the naked eye on the night of November 15th

00:04:53.937 --> 00:04:54.677
and 16th.

00:04:55.137 --> 00:05:01.017
Researchers, according to Fizz.org, are evaluating a common food additive to keep solid particles

00:05:01.017 --> 00:05:05.877
suspended in gelled fuels, addressing a long-standing hybrid propellant challenge.

00:05:05.877 --> 00:05:12.077
Astronaut Koichi Wakata, according to GMA Network, highlighted research, manufacturing,

00:05:12.437 --> 00:05:17.217
and human spaceflight prospects for ASEAN partners as commercial access to orbit

00:05:17.217 --> 00:05:17.557
grows.

00:05:18.685 --> 00:05:23.825
Matt Cook, according to the telegraph in Argus, used to explain galaxy formation on building

00:05:23.825 --> 00:05:28.585
sites in Bradford, and now leads the UK Space Agency's exploration program.

00:05:29.025 --> 00:05:33.905
He heads work on astronauts, lunar missions, and the Rosalind Franklin Mars rover built

00:05:33.905 --> 00:05:34.705
in Stevenage.

00:05:34.705 --> 00:05:38.765
He attended the Artemis II launch, which he described as a life highlight.

00:05:39.265 --> 00:05:43.905
Cook credits his multicultural hometown with preparing him for international collaboration,

00:05:44.445 --> 00:05:48.045
and urges young people not to self-reject when applying for space roles.

00:05:48.994 --> 00:05:53.694
Gravity can send light from a single explosion down several curved paths around a massive

00:05:53.694 --> 00:05:58.454
galaxy cluster, so each image arrives at a different time because the light has traveled

00:05:58.454 --> 00:05:59.294
a different distance.

00:05:59.894 --> 00:06:04.534
That delay comes from extra path length on the longer routes, which can grow to years

00:06:04.534 --> 00:06:07.474
or decades, even though the blast itself happened once.

00:06:08.254 --> 00:06:12.574
Repeated high-resolution images over the coming decades are still required to check the

00:06:12.574 --> 00:06:17.494
predicted arrival windows and to see how closely the cluster's mass distribution fits the models.

00:06:18.134 --> 00:06:23.174
The same future images would test whether small tweaks to the lens model change the expected delays.

00:06:24.217 --> 00:06:29.017
Oxygen leaking from dwarf galaxies, gravitational waves from white dwarf pairs,

00:06:29.437 --> 00:06:33.417
and Euclid's giant galaxy map were the throughlines of the past seven days.

00:06:33.417 --> 00:06:39.297
Dwarf galaxies keep only 15% of the oxygen their stars produce, and that is still where

00:06:39.297 --> 00:06:40.337
that result stands.

00:06:40.817 --> 00:06:45.217
Tight white dwarf pairs could give away hidden exoplanets across the Milky Way through

00:06:45.217 --> 00:06:49.037
gravitational wave frequency sidebands that Lisa can detect.

00:06:49.557 --> 00:06:55.937
Euclid put out images covering 900 million galaxies across a patch equal to 9,500 full moons,

00:06:56.297 --> 00:06:57.737
and that map is now public.

